Z||000008||stsmithgt||02-15-2001||01:54 PM||||It's better they tell you now then after you've sunk a bunch of money into it.  My folks forced me to sell a car back in highschool because they said I was putting too much money into it.  With the 2 week time limit they gave me, I sold it for way less than what I rebuilt just the engine for.  Consider yourself lucky and set new plans and goals you think your parents won't interupt.  Mine said sell it or move out...I wasn't ready to move out. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000009||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||02-17-2001||04:38 AM||||I say move out.  Let your parents explain to the state why a minor is living outside the home.  They can say "Junior spent too much on his car . . ."<P>Your parents care about you growing up right.  They should encourage cars as they take all your time and money.  Less time and money for beers, drugs, and parties.  If you are wrenching, then that's time which you won't be spending making unexpected children and single mommies.<P>Tell your parents that the car is the only teen obsession that you can get over when you grow up.  Babies on the other hand are a lifetime commitment.  Drugs are somewhere in the middle.  Beer and alcohol can mean the end of your life in many ways.<P>A Fiero can cost you a little money, lots of time, and maybe some tickets.  When it's all over, you still have a nice car to drool on.  <P>Also, wrenching teaches self-reliance, ingnuity, and basic troubleshooting skills.  It's the way most engineers start out on their well paying careers.  I am sure your Mom and Dad are looking out for what's best for you, but this time they seem to have let you down.  I am truly sorry.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||William Federle||02-17-2001||07:29 AM||||Maybe they'll let you have a hobby - if you had a place for a cradle you could work on putting a v8 on it - and later when things loosen up you'd be ready to install it in a donor car. Just an idea I had.<P><BR>||||reg||1
